## Section III: T+1 Settlement Compression Mechanics & Systemic Vulnerability Vectors (Post-May 2024 Audit)

On May 28, 2024, the SEC formally implemented amended **Rule 15c6-1**, transitioning the U.S. cash equity, corporate bond, and municipal securities markets from a $T+2$ to a $T+1$ settlement cycle.

```
========================================================================================================================
 T+2 vs. T+1 POST-TRADE OPERATIONAL TIMELINE COMPARISON
========================================================================================================================

T+2 SETTLEMENT TIMELINE (Legacy):
Trade Date (T)                         T+1                                             Settlement Date (T+2)
[Trade Execution] ──────────────────── [Allocations & Confirmations] ───────────────── [12-Hour Reconciliation] ──> [Settlement]

T+1 SETTLEMENT TIMELINE (Current):
Trade Date (T)                                                         Settlement Date (T+1)
[Trade Execution] ──> [Allocations (7:00 PM)] ──> [Affirmation (9:00 PM)] ──> [CNS Night Cycle (11:30 PM)] ──> [Final NSS (4:15 PM)]
                      |<──────── 2-Hour Window (83% Compression) ──────>|
========================================================================================================================

```

### 1. Operational Window Compression Mechanics

* **Post-Trade Window Reduction:** Compresses the post-trade operational reconciliation window from approximately **12 hours down to 2 hours** (an **83% reduction** in available error-resolution time).
* **Affirmation Deadline (9:00 PM ET):** Institutional trade allocations must be finalized by 7:00 PM ET on trade date ($T$), and trade affirmations must be submitted to the DTCC Trade Clearing (ICTC) matching utility by **9:00 PM ET on $T$**.
* **Night-Cycle Processing (11:30 PM ET):** Affirmed trades enter the NSCC’s CNS Night Cycle at 11:30 PM ET on $T$, where multilateral netting and inventory assignment occur prior to the morning settlement cycle. Unaffirmed trades drop out of automated CNS processing and must be settled via gross, manual matching on $T+1$, increasing counterparty risk.

